{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790995929,"nonce":"IAII70EFwJY5jSYxCU0NpWenE7A=","title":"poll pumps the platform each time its window's queue is empty","message":"Window.poll pumped winit whenever the window had nothing queued. On macOS a pump with a zero timeout runs NSApp.run() once and can take a display frame, so a program that drains each frame with wait and then poll until None pumped again every time the queue emptied. Under steady input every pump brought more, and the drain ran as long as the input did.\n\nReported by ashui (2026-10-03): trackpad scrolling over a window gave 21,658 events at 2.6 ms per poll; one frame's 751 events took 1,317 ms to drain, and moving the pointer over empty space used 50-90% of a core. Device events, which ashui never asked for, were nearly half the traffic.\n\nReproduced on an M1 Pro with synthetic CGEvents (moves and scroll steps, 3 s within a 4 s drain): before, 1.82 ms per poll, a worst frame drain of 2,969 ms and 78% of a core; after, a poll costs microseconds, no drain is measurable, and 8%. Under Ash through hlwindow, 0.002 ms per poll including the Haxe getters. Idle, wait sleeps in the OS (3-4% for a loop waking at 60 Hz).\n\nFixed in 12362a9f367237bc62a231acff59e8e82a0a75ed: a poll pumps only when its window has answered none since the last pump, so a frame's drain pumps once, in its wait. Window.listenDeviceEvents(Always | WhenFocused | Never) chooses when raw device events come; Never drops them.","files":null}]}